user information is stored in /etc/passwd
password information is stored in /etc/shadow in hashed values
group information is stored in /etc/group
when creating or editing users or groups, /etc/login.defs provides default configuration information such as UID or password policy 
When a user is created with a home directory, Linux copies files from /etc/skel. These files define the default shell environment for new users.
difference of /etc/skel and /etc/login.defs:
/etc/skel is a directory: template for a new user's home directory, controls files copied to new home directories,say, copy from /etc/skel/welcome.txt to /home/tester1/welcome.txt,
used when creating a user's home directory
/etc/login.defs: a configuration file, default settings for user/account management, controls things like UID ranges, password aging, etc. used when useradd / login-related account behavior


SuperUser access:
sudo permissions are defined in /etc/sudoers or /etc/sudoers.d/*
in the file:
username ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL                      //this user can run all commands using sudo without a password prompt
%groupname ALL=(ALL) ALL                             //this group can run all commands using sudo
username ALL=(ALL) ALL, !/sbin/useradd               //this user can run all commands using sudo except "useradd"; 2nd field: hosts, 3rd field: commands, 4th field: commands excepted
$ visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/file1                     //if file1 is not existing, -f is creating it; files in /etc/sudoers.d/ are read only, that's why use visudo instead of vi to edit them
//contents in file1
alice ALL=(ALL) ALL
bob ALL=(ALL) /usr/sbin/useradd, /usr/sbin/userdel
carol ALL=(ALL) ALL, !/usr/sbin/userdel
%opsadmins ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL
