a user can belong to different groups, one would be the primary group, other groups are called supplementary groups
$ usermod -G group1, group2 user1                    //-G replaces previous supplementary groups that person is in 
$ usermod -aG group3 user1                           //-aG adds groups user1 into group3
$ id user1                                           //verify
$ usermod -g group4 user1                            //-g replaces primary group

$ groupmod -n newname oldname                        //-n change the group name
$ groupmod -g 20000 groupname                        //-g change group ID                 
$ cat /etc/group                                     //verify



passwords management:
for newly created accounts, default setting is confgiured in /etc/login.defs
for existing accounts, use 'chage' command to adjust password expiration, enforce change intervals and control account aging
$ chage user                                         //change password expiration setting                                    
$ chage-h                                            //--help
$ chage -l user                                      //list password information 
$ chage -d 0 user                                    //change password upon first login for the user, -d 0 sets the last password change date to 0
$ chage -E 2027-01-15 user                           //set an expiry date for the user
$ passwd user                                        //change password for the user
$ passwd                                             //change password for current user
$ chage -M 30 user                                   //change password policy of the user, -M: maximum, make the expiration of the password 30 days
$ chage -m 7 user                                    //wait 7 days before changing the password again
$ chage -W 5 user                                    //warn the user 5 days before the expiration
4 chage -l user                                      //list password pilicies of the user

Q & A:
Create a service account called backupsvc with a restricted login shell that prevents interactive login. Verify the shell configuration in /etc/passwd.
$ sudo useradd -s /sbin/nologin backupsvc
$ grep backupsvc /etc/passwd

Modify developer2's login shell to /bin/sh. Verify the change by checking /etc/passwd.
$ sudo usermod -s /bin/sh developer2
$ grep developer2 /etc/passwd

Set passwords for developer1 account. Set a simple password like TestPass123 for testing purposes.
$ sudo bash -c 'echo "developer1:TestPass123" | chpasswd'

Configure global password policy in /etc/login.defs with:
PASS_MAX_DAYS   90
PASS_MIN_DAYS   1
PASS_WARN_AGE   7
Verify the settings were applied.
$ sudo sed -i 's/^PASS_MAX_DAYS.*/PASS_MAX_DAYS   90/' /etc/login.defs
$ sudo sed -i 's/^PASS_MIN_DAYS.*/PASS_MIN_DAYS   1/' /etc/login.defs
$ sudo sed -i 's/^PASS_WARN_AGE.*/PASS_WARN_AGE   7/' /etc/login.defs
$ grep 'PASS_MAX_DAYS\|PASS_MIN_DAYS\|PASS_WARN_AGE' /etc/login.defs


